S[106] = "6.2 Identification and credibility"
S[108] = ("__Wealth-side constraint.__ The joint loss penalises both production residuals and deviations from CWON produced capital, which is compiled from national balance sheets with its own asset lives, revaluation conventions and depreciation schedules. On the estimation grid, the joint objective is less flat than the production-only objective in most countries, but countries with thin CWON coverage retain a broad ridge, and we flag them.")
S[109] = ("__Cross-country variation.__ The relational slope ⟦ρ_{2}⟧ rises with R&D intensity (slope 0.068, t = 2.34 under M4; Supplementary Figure 3), consistent with ⟦β⟧ capturing a component correlated with, but not mechanically determined by, measured R&D spending.")
S[110] = ("__Depreciation.__ Across six alternative depreciation schedules, the median and mean M1 lag stay within 2% of their baseline values (Supplementary Figure 12), so under our grid protocol the lag is not simply an artefact of depreciation mismeasurement, although the two channels are confounded in a single-equation setting.")
S[111] = ("None of these arguments is a structural identification proof of the kind available from natural experiments or instrumental variables. The framework is a calibrated, assumption-conditional decomposition disciplined by the requirement that flow and stock accounts agree. Whether alternative parameter configurations could generate the same observables remains open and requires richer asset-level data.")
S[112] = "6.3 Implications for measurement practice"
S[113] = None
S[114] = ("Three low-cost steps follow for statistical practice: reporting the assumed investment-to-service lag alongside depreciation in PIM documentation; publishing a sensitivity estimate of broad intangible coverage next to produced-capital aggregates; and using the relational diagnostic (⟦ρ_{1}⟧, ⟦ρ_{2}⟧) as a routine consistency check between PIM and wealth-account stocks. For cross-country productivity comparisons (e.g. Inklaar and Timmer, 2013), differences in investment composition imply that a common zero-lag convention may affect relative TFP levels; reporting results under alternative lag assumptions would make this sensitivity visible.")
