S[121] = "6.5 Limitations"
S[123] = "7. Conclusion"
S[124] = ("The parameters that link flow and stock accounts, namely the investment-to-service lag and the intangible share, are usually imposed rather than estimated. When the lag is constructed from observable investment composition, the 2010–2019 capital stock is a median 4.3% lower than the standard PIM stock in 35 economies, and measured log-level TFP and the implied labour share are about 1.7 percentage points higher. These are measurement consequences conditional on calibrated asset lags, not forecasting improvements: the out-of-sample gain from a time-varying lag is not statistically significant. The joint flow–stock model improves a strict wealth-account holdout, but it is a consistency device rather than a predictive model.")
S[125] = ("The adjustments are modest on average but reach −7.1% for the Republic of Korea and −9.2% for Ireland, where investment composition changed most. Because the observable lag requires only OECD asset-composition data, it offers statistical agencies and researchers an inexpensive sensitivity check on capital, TFP and labour-share comparisons across countries.")
